---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import VLMComparison      from "../../../design-system/components/shared/VLMComparison.jsx";
import VLMHeadlineSamples from "../../../design-system/components/shared/VLMHeadlineSamples.jsx";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="VLM extraction · TV news multimodal extraction"
  description="One-shot ticker extraction with vision-language models, compared head-to-head with the OCR pipeline."
>
  {/* hero */}
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-12">
      <span class="pill pill-visual mb-4">Visual path · alternative</span>
      <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        One model, one call, one JSON of headlines
      </h1>
      <p class="prose-thesis mt-4 max-w-2xl">
        Instead of detecting glyphs, recognizing text, and segmenting the OCR
        stream, hand each stitched panorama tile straight to a vision-language
        model and ask it to emit a list of complete headlines. Same input
        tiles, same prompt, same aggregator as the OCR branch — only the
        recognizer changes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* setup callout */}
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 py-12 grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="card p-6">
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
          Input
        </div>
        <div class="text-lg font-semibold mb-1">1,845 tiles</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
          The same 75 v6 panoramas, sliced into 3,000-pixel tiles with
          1,000-pixel overlap so headlines straddling a boundary are intact
          in at least one tile.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
          Prompt
        </div>
        <div class="text-lg font-semibold mb-1">Byte-identical</div>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
          One JSON-mode instruction telling the model to emit only headlines
          whose first and last words land cleanly inside the tile. Shared
          across every model so quality differences are model-attributable.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
          Output
        </div>
        <div class="text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
          <code class="mono text-sm">headlines_combined.json</code>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
          Same schema as <code class="mono">news_items_cleaned.json</code>,
          so the nine LLM summarizers consume VLM output without knowing
          the difference.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* model cards + table */}
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2">Three recognizers, one task</h2>
      <p class="prose-thesis max-w-3xl mb-8">
        The OCR pipeline is the established baseline. Gemini 2.5 Flash is
        the original paid VLM run. Ministral 3 14B is an
        <strong> open-weights</strong> vision model served free through
        Mistral La Plateforme — the answer to "does the comparison change
        when the VLM costs zero?"
      </p>

      <VLMComparison client:load reportUrl="/data/vlm/vlm_opensource_evaluation.json" />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* what the numbers mean */}
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-6">Reading the table</h2>
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
            Recall
          </div>
          <div class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2 text-visual">100% · both VLMs</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
            Every ground-truth headline appears somewhere in the VLM output.
            The VLM nearly never <em>misses</em>; both recover everything OCR
            does and then some.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
            Precision
          </div>
          <div class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2">VLM emits too much</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
            Mistral emits 3,214 candidate headlines on the 14h video; only a
            fraction match GT. The OCR pipeline pays its $0.05 LLM-cleanup
            cost precisely to push precision past 84%.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-text-subtle mb-2">
            Cost
          </div>
          <div class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2">€8.28 → $0.00</div>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted">
            The paid Gemini VLM run cost <strong>€8.28 in total</strong>
            (actual Google billing — the code's per-run token estimate of
            ~$0.87 under-reported it and is unverified). Swapping Gemini for
            the open-weights Ministral 3 14B drops cost to literally zero
            and <em>raises</em> F1 by ~6 points. The free VLM still trails
            OCR, but it's the cheapest way to get 100% recall.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* headline samples */}
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2">First 20 headlines from each VLM</h2>
      <p class="prose-thesis max-w-3xl mb-8">
        The same tiles, same prompt, same JSON schema — only the model
        weights differ. Gemini paraphrases and stitches; Ministral repeats
        and over-emits. Both, in their own way, capture every real headline.
      </p>
      <VLMHeadlineSamples client:load />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* method footnote */}
  <section>
    <div class="max-w-content mx-auto px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Method</h2>
      <ul class="prose-thesis max-w-3xl space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          Tiles are produced by <code class="mono">vlm_extraction/tiler.py</code>,
          cached under <code class="mono">vlm_extraction/output/_tiles/</code>;
          every model sees byte-identical inputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          The prompt lives in <code class="mono">vlm_extraction/prompts.py</code>
          and is sent unmodified to every model.
        </li>
        <li>
          Metrics come from <code class="mono">validation/validate_extraction.py</code>
          at a 70% fuzzy-match threshold, the same threshold the OCR pipeline
          is reported at.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hallucination = fraction of emitted headlines that match no GT
          headline even at the loosest 60% threshold.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ministral 3 14B is the official open-weights successor Mistral
          recommends after Pixtral 12B was retired on 2025-12-31. It runs
          on the Free Experiment plan: 1 B tokens per month, no card.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
